INT. FUNICULAR CAR - MID-SLOPE - NIGHT

Stalled. Wooden benches, brass rails. Outside, black pines and a thousand feet of dark. Wind rocks the car on its cable.

AUGUST MWANGI, 40s, charcoal coat, gloved hands folded, sits very still. Tall, calm, eyes of a chess player.

Across from him, SILAS DRAKEFORD, 40s, wet hair, a bruise ripening on his cheekbone, grips a leather briefcase to his chest.

SILAS
You pulled the brake.

AUGUST
I pulled nothing.

SILAS
Then who did?

AUGUST
The cable did what cables do.

Silas shifts. His hand slides toward his pocket. August watches it.

AUGUST (CONT'D)
Don't.

SILAS
I wasn't.

AUGUST
Then he wouldn't have said I was.

SILAS
Who?

AUGUST
You know who.

Silas swallows. His eyes dart to the window. Nothing but pine and fog.

SILAS
He told you I took it.

AUGUST
He told me you were smart. And that smart men carry briefcases.

Silas laughs, brittle. He sets the briefcase on his knees. Clasps it shut.

A scrape. On the roof. Both men look up.

AUGUST (CONT'D)
Deer.

SILAS
On a funicular?

Another scrape, heavier. The car sways.

The roof hatch slams open. A boot drops through. Then a leg. Then a woman, ZOFIA BRÅTEN, 30s, climbing gear, ice axe, headlamp blazing. She lands in a crouch, axe leveled.

ZOFIA
Nobody move.

Both men raise their hands. She sweeps the light across them, lands on August.

ZOFIA (CONT'D)
You. Open the briefcase.

AUGUST
It's his.

ZOFIA
(to Silas)
Then you.

Silas hesitates. His hand shakes. He unlatches. Flips the lid.

Inside, nothing. Foam padding, cut to hold something that's no longer there.

ZOFIA (CONT'D)
Where is it?

SILAS
I don't know.

August smiles. Slowly lowers his hands.

AUGUST
He knows.

He nods at the window. On the glass, a small hand-shaped print. On the outside.

The car lurches. Falls ten feet and catches.

All three scream.
